# SYNOPSIS

```usage
tar [x | t | -x | -t] [-C dir] [-J | -Z | -a | -j | -z] [-m] [-p]
    [-f file] [file ...]
tar [c | -c] [-C dir] [-J | -Z | -a | -j | -z] [-h] path ... [-f file]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`tar` makes archives in POSIX's ustar format, lists them, and extracts files from them.
The first argument may name the action without a `-`, and carry flags after it, as in `tar xvf a.tar`.

: `c`, `-c`
  Create an archive of each <path>, and of everything beneath it if it is a directory, a directory before its contents.
  Names are stored as given; a name of 100 bytes or more is stored in a GNU long-name record before its header.
  The archive itself is left out, with a warning, if it is among what is archived.
: `t`, `-t`
  List the name of each member of the archive.
: `x`, `-x`
  Extract each member of the archive, or, with <file>s, each member whose name is a <file> or lies beneath one.
  A file that is in the way is removed first, and missing directories are made.
  Each member's mode and modification time are restored, and its owner and group, since the C library gives every process user ID 0.
  Long names in GNU long-name records and the `path` of pax extended headers are read; pax global headers are skipped.

: `-f` <file>
  Read or write the archive <file>; with none, or a <file> starting with `-`, standard input or standard output.
: `-C` <dir>
  Change to <dir> before archiving or extracting.
: `-h`
  Follow symbolic links when archiving, storing what they point to.
: `-m`
  Do not restore modification times.
: `-p`
  Accepted and ignored: modes are always restored.
: `-v`
  Write each name as it is archived or extracted.
: `-J`, `-Z`, `-a`, `-j`, `-z`
  Pass the archive through `xz`, `compress`, `lzma`, `bzip2` or `gzip` (see BUGS).

`tar` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/tar`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/tar`.
There is no native `tar`.
POSIX's archiver is `pax`, which sbase does not have.

